    47→    <header class="docs-header">
    48→        <h1>Core Rendering</h1>
    49→        <p class="subtitle">Edge Rasterization, Shape Drawing, and Blend Modes</p>
    50→    </header>
    51→
    52→    <main class="docs-content">
    53→        <section class="intro-section">
    54→            <h2>Introduction</h2>
    55→            <p>
    56→                Core rendering forms the foundation of the entire graphics engine. Every shape drawn on screen—whether a simple rectangle or a complex curved path—ultimately becomes a collection of edges that are rasterized into pixels through a scanline algorithm.
    57→            </p>
    58→            <p>
    59→                This section establishes the fundamental building blocks: drawing rectangles, implementing edge-based rasterization, and creating a full Porter-Duff alpha blending system. It then extends this foundation to handle arbitrary convex polygons with viewport clipping, enabling the rendering of triangles, pentagons, and any other convex shape.
    60→            </p>
    61→            <p>
    62→                This page covers the core algorithms that make it all work: how shapes are converted to edges, how those edges are walked scanline-by-scanline, how alpha values are properly composited, and how shapes are clipped to stay within the viewport boundaries.
    63→            </p>
    64→        </section>
    65→
    66→        <div class="tabs-container" role="tablist" aria-label="Core rendering topics">
    67→            <div class="tabs-header">
    68→                <button id="tab-edge" class="tab-button active" role="tab" aria-selected="true" aria-controls="panel-edge">
    69→                    Edge Rasterization
    70→                </button>
    71→                <button id="tab-rect" class="tab-button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="panel-rect">
    72→                    Rectangle Drawing
    73→                </button>
    74→                <button id="tab-blend" class="tab-button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="panel-blend">
    75→                    Porter-Duff Blending
    76→                </button>
    77→                <button id="tab-clip" class="tab-button" role="tab" aria-selected="false" aria-controls="panel-clip">
    78→                    Polygon Clipping
    79→                </button>
    80→            </div>
    81→
    82→            <div class="tabs-content">
    83→                <!-- Tab 1: Edge Rasterization -->
    84→                <div id="panel-edge" class="tab-panel active" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-edge">
    85→                    <h3>Edge-Based Rendering</h3>
    86→                    <p>
    87→                        The graphics engine uses an edge-based scanline algorithm for all shape rendering. Every shape—rectangle, triangle, polygon, path—is first converted into a list of edges. Each edge represents one side of the shape, storing the top and bottom Y coordinates, the X position, and the slope (how X changes as Y increments).
    88→                    </p>
    89→                    <p>
    90→                        The scanline algorithm then processes the frame buffer row by row. For each Y coordinate, it finds all active edges, calculates their X intersections, and fills between pairs of edges. This approach handles arbitrary shapes efficiently and naturally supports anti-aliasing in future extensions.
    91→                    </p>
    92→
    93→                    <h4>Edge Structure</h4>
    94→                    <p>
    95→                        Each edge stores its vertical span (top to bottom), its current X position, and its slope for efficient scanline traversal. The <code>dire</code> (direction) field tracks whether the edge runs left-to-right or right-to-left, which is crucial for the winding rule used in complex path rendering.
    96→                    </p>
    97→
    98→                    <pre class="line-numbers" data-line="13-14,29-32,38-39,244-245"><code class="language-cpp">class Edge {
    99→public:
   100→    int top, bottom;
   101→    float m, x;
   102→    int dire;
   103→    float left_x;
   104→
   105→    Edge(GPoint p0, GPoint p1, GBitmap fDevice, int dir) {
   106→       if (p0.y &gt; p1.y) {
   107→            std::swap(p0, p1);
   108→        }
   109→        top = GRoundToInt(p0.y);
   110→        bottom = GRoundToInt(p1.y);
   111→        left_x = p0.x;
   112→        m = calculateSlope(p0, p1);
   113→
   114→        float b = calculateB(p0, p1);
   115→        x = b;
   116→
   117→        dire = dir;
   118→    }
   119→
   120→    float eval(int y){
   121→        return m * ((float)y + 0.5f) + x;
   122→    }
   123→
   124→private:
   125→    float calculateSlope(GPoint p0, GPoint p1) {
   126→        return (p1.x - p0.x) / (p1.y - p0.y);
   127→    }
   128→
   129→    float calculateB(GPoint p0, GPoint p1) {
   130→        return p0.x - (m * p0.y);
   131→    }
   132→};</code></pre>
   133→
   134→                    <h4>Scanline Filling</h4>
   135→                    <p>
   136→                        The <code>eval()</code> method is the heart of edge rasterization. For any given Y coordinate, it calculates the corresponding X position by evaluating <code>m * (y + 0.5) + b</code>. The <code>+ 0.5</code> samples at the pixel center, which is essential for proper geometric accuracy.
   137→                    </p>
   138→                    <p>
   139→                        During rendering, edges are sorted by Y position, then processed scanline by scanline. As each row is rendered, the algorithm finds pairs of edge intersections and fills the pixels between them. This approach works for any convex polygon and forms the foundation for more complex path rendering.
   140→                    </p>
   141→                </div>
   142→
   143→                <!-- Tab 2: Rectangle Drawing -->
   144→                <div id="panel-rect" class="tab-panel" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-rect">
   145→                    <h3>Rectangle Rendering</h3>
   146→                    <p>
   147→                        Rectangles are the simplest renderable shape, but they demonstrate the core rendering pipeline. A rectangle is defined by four corner points, which are converted into four edges, then passed through the same scanline algorithm used for all shapes.
   148→                    </p>
   149→                    <p>
   150→                        The <code>drawRect()</code> function converts a <code>GRect</code> into four <code>GPoint</code> vertices, then delegates to <code>drawConvexPolygon()</code>. This design means rectangles get all the benefits of the polygon renderer: clipping, transformation support, and complex blend modes.
   151→                    </p>
   152→
   153→                    <h4>Rectangle to Polygon Conversion</h4>
   154→                    <pre class="line-numbers" data-line="40-46"><code class="language-cpp">void MyCanvas::drawRect(const GRect&amp; rect, const GPaint&amp; paint) {
   155→
   156→    GPoint p1 = {rect.left, rect.top};
   157→    GPoint p2 = {rect.right, rect.top};
   158→    GPoint p3 = {rect.right, rect.bottom};
   159→    GPoint p4 = {rect.left, rect.bottom};
   160→
   161→    GPoint src[4] = {p1, p2, p3, p4};
   162→    drawConvexPolygon(src, 4, paint);
   163→};</code></pre>
   164→
   165→                    <h4>Convex Polygon Rendering</h4>
   166→                    <p>
   167→                        The <code>drawConvexPolygon()</code> function handles all convex shapes. It transforms points by the current transformation matrix, creates edges between consecutive vertices (wrapping from the last back to the first), clips those edges to the viewport, sorts them by Y coordinate, and finally fills the polygon using the scanline algorithm.
   168→                    </p>
   169→
   170→                    <pre class="line-numbers" data-line="56,58,62-66,73-75"><code class="language-cpp">void MyCanvas::drawConvexPolygon(const GPoint points[], int count, const GPaint&amp; paint){
   171→
   172→    if(count &lt; 3){
   173→        return;
   174→    }
   175→
   176→    GPoint dst[count];
   177→    GMatrix mat = ctm[ctm.size() - 1];
   178→
   179→    mat.mapPoints(dst, points, count);
   180→
   181→    std::vector&lt;Edge&gt; edges;
   182→
   183→    // Create array of edges
   184→    for(int i = 0; i &lt; count; i++){
   185→        int nextIndex = (i + 1) % count;
   186→        Edge::clip(dst[i], dst[nextIndex], fDevice, edges);
   187→    }
   188→
   189→    if (edges.size() &lt; 2){
   190→        return;
   191→    }
   192→
   193→    // Sort by top value
   194→    std::sort(edges.begin(), edges.end(), [](const Edge&amp; a, const Edge&amp; b) {
   195→        return a.top &gt; b.top;
   196→    });
   197→
   198→    // ... blend mode selection and optimization ...
   199→
   200→    fillPolygon(edges, color, proc, fDevice);
   201→}</code></pre>
   202→
   203→                    <p>
   204→                        Note how the function creates edges by connecting each vertex to the next, with the last vertex wrapping back to the first via modulo arithmetic. This automatically closes the polygon. The edges are then clipped and sorted before being passed to the fill routine.
   205→                    </p>
   206→                </div>
   207→
   208→                <!-- Tab 3: Porter-Duff Blending -->
   209→                <div id="panel-blend" class="tab-panel" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-blend">
   210→                    <h3>Alpha Compositing with Porter-Duff</h3>
   211→                    <p>
   212→                        The graphics engine implements the complete Porter-Duff compositing model with 12 blend modes. Porter-Duff blending defines how source pixels (S) combine with destination pixels (D) based on their alpha values, using the formula: <code>result = S_coef * S + D_coef * D</code>.
   213→                    </p>
   214→                    <p>
   215→                        All blending operates on <strong>premultiplied alpha</strong>, where RGB channels are already multiplied by the alpha channel. This representation makes compositing math simpler and more efficient: instead of unpremultiply-blend-repremultiply on every pixel, we work directly with premultiplied values.
   216→                    </p>
   217→
   218→                    <h4>Src Over (Most Common Blend Mode)</h4>
   219→                    <p>
   220→                        The <code>src_over</code> blend mode is the default compositing operation used when drawing one image over another. It places the source pixel "over" the destination, with the destination showing through transparent areas. The formula is: <code>S + (1 - Sa) * D</code>, where Sa is the source alpha.
   221→                    </p>
   222→
   223→                    <pre class="line-numbers" data-line="35-38"><code class="language-cpp">// S + (1 - Sa)*D
   224→GPixel src_over_mode(GPixel src, GPixel dest){
   225→    int sa = GPixel_GetA(src);
   226→    int sr = GPixel_GetR(src);
   227→    int sg = GPixel_GetG(src);
   228→    int sb = GPixel_GetB(src);
   229→
   230→    int da = GPixel_GetA(dest);
   231→    int dr = GPixel_GetR(dest);
   232→    int dg = GPixel_GetG(dest);
   233→    int db = GPixel_GetB(dest);
   234→
   235→
   236→    int ba = sa + div255((255-sa)*da);
   237→    int br = sr + div255((255-sa)*dr);
   238→    int bg = sg + div255((255-sa)*dg);
   239→    int bb = sb + div255((255-sa)*db);
   240→
   241→    return GPixel_PackARGB(ba, br, bg, bb);
   242→}</code></pre>
   243→
   244→                    <p>
   245→                        The <code>div255()</code> helper function performs fast fixed-point division by 255, essential for performance since blending happens per-pixel. The alpha math <code>sa + div255((255-sa)*da)</code> produces the final alpha: fully opaque where source is opaque, blending source and destination alpha elsewhere.
   246→                    </p>
   247→
   248→                    <h4>Other Porter-Duff Modes</h4>
   249→                    <p>
   250→                        The engine supports all 12 standard Porter-Duff operators. Here are two more examples:
   251→                    </p>
   252→
   253→                    <pre class="line-numbers"><code class="language-cpp">// D + (1 - Da)*S
   254→GPixel dst_over_mode(GPixel src, GPixel dest){
   255→    int sa = GPixel_GetA(src);
   256→    int sr = GPixel_GetR(src);
   257→    int sg = GPixel_GetG(src);
   258→    int sb = GPixel_GetB(src);
   259→
   260→    int da = GPixel_GetA(dest);
   261→    int dr = GPixel_GetR(dest);
   262→    int dg = GPixel_GetG(dest);
   263→    int db = GPixel_GetB(dest);
   264→
   265→
   266→    int ba = da + div255((255-da)*sa);
   267→    int br = dr + div255((255-da)*sr);
   268→    int bg = dg + div255((255-da)*sg);
   269→    int bb = db + div255((255-da)*sb);
   270→
   271→    return GPixel_PackARGB(ba, br, bg, bb);
   272→}
   273→
   274→// (1 - Sa)*D + (1 - Da)*S
   275→GPixel xor_mode(GPixel src, GPixel dest){
   276→    int sa = GPixel_GetA(src);
   277→    int sr = GPixel_GetR(src);
   278→    int sg = GPixel_GetG(src);
   279→    int sb = GPixel_GetB(src);
   280→
   281→    int da = GPixel_GetA(dest);
   282→    int dr = GPixel_GetR(dest);
   283→    int dg = GPixel_GetG(dest);
   284→    int db = GPixel_GetB(dest);
   285→
   286→    int ba = div255((255-sa)*da) + div255((255-da)*sa);
   287→    int br = div255((255-sa)*dr) + div255((255-da)*sr);
   288→    int bg = div255((255-sa)*dg) + div255((255-da)*sg);
   289→    int bb = div255((255-sa)*db) + div255((255-da)*sb);
   290→
   291→    return GPixel_PackARGB(ba, br, bg, bb);
   292→}</code></pre>
   293→
   294→                    <h4>Complete Blend Mode List</h4>
   295→                    <p>The engine implements these 12 Porter-Duff operators:</p>
   296→                    <ul>
   297→                        <li><strong>clear</strong>: Result is transparent (0)</li>
   298→                        <li><strong>src</strong>: Source replaces destination (S)</li>
   299→                        <li><strong>dst</strong>: Destination unchanged (D)</li>
   300→                        <li><strong>src_over</strong>: Source over destination (S + (1-Sa)*D)</li>
   301→                        <li><strong>dst_over</strong>: Destination over source (D + (1-Da)*S)</li>
   302→                        <li><strong>src_in</strong>: Source inside destination (Da*S)</li>
   303→                        <li><strong>dst_in</strong>: Destination inside source (Sa*D)</li>
   304→                        <li><strong>src_out</strong>: Source outside destination ((1-Da)*S)</li>
   305→                        <li><strong>dst_out</strong>: Destination outside source ((1-Sa)*D)</li>
   306→                        <li><strong>src_atop</strong>: Source atop destination (Da*S + (1-Sa)*D)</li>
   307→                        <li><strong>dst_atop</strong>: Destination atop source (Sa*D + (1-Da)*S)</li>
   308→                        <li><strong>xor</strong>: Exclusive or ((1-Sa)*D + (1-Da)*S)</li>
   309→                    </ul>
   310→                </div>
   311→
   312→                <!-- Tab 4: Polygon Clipping -->
   313→                <div id="panel-clip" class="tab-panel" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="tab-clip">
   314→                    <h3>Viewport Clipping</h3>
   315→                    <p>
   316→                        Clipping ensures that shapes extending outside the viewport boundaries are properly trimmed before rasterization. Without clipping, edge calculations could produce invalid memory accesses or visual artifacts. The engine clips each edge of a polygon against the viewport's four boundaries: top, bottom, left, and right.
   317→                    </p>
   318→                    <p>
   319→                        The <code>Edge::clip()</code> function takes two points defining an edge and clips them against the bitmap dimensions. It handles all cases: edges completely inside, completely outside, and partially clipped. When an edge crosses a boundary, the function calculates the intersection point and creates clipped edges that stay within bounds.
   320→                    </p>
   321→
   322→                    <h4>Vertical Clipping</h4>
   323→                    <p>
   324→                        First, the function clips against top and bottom boundaries. If an edge is completely above or below the viewport, it's discarded. If it crosses a boundary, the function calculates the X coordinate where the edge intersects the boundary and creates a new point at that intersection.
   325→                    </p>
   326→
   327→                    <pre class="line-numbers" data-line="66-78"><code class="language-cpp">static void clip(GPoint p0, GPoint p1, GBitmap fDevice, std::vector&lt;Edge&gt; &amp;edges){
   328→
   329→    int w = p0.y &gt; p1.y;
   330→    int dir = 2*w - 1;
   331→    if (p0.y &gt; p1.y) {
   332→        std::swap(p0, p1);
   333→    }
   334→
   335→    GPoint new_p0 = p0;
   336→    GPoint new_p1 = p1;
   337→
   338→    // Line segment is completely out of bounds
   339→    if (p1.y &lt; 0 || p0.y &gt;= fDevice.height()) {
   340→        return;
   341→    }
   342→
   343→    // Line segment top point is out of bounds
   344→    if (p0.y &lt; 0){
   345→        new_p0 = {getTopX(p0, p1), 0};
   346→    }
   347→
   348→    // Line segment bottom point is out of bounds
   349→    if (p1.y &gt; fDevice.height()){
   350→        new_p1 = {getBottomX(p0, p1, fDevice), float(fDevice.height())};
   351→    }
   352→
   353→    // Reassign p0 and p1 with new x values
   354→    p0 = new_p0;
   355→    p1 = new_p1;
   356→
   357→    // ... horizontal clipping continues ...
   358→}</code></pre>
   359→
   360→                    <h4>Horizontal Clipping Cases</h4>
   361→                    <p>
   362→                        After vertical clipping, the function handles horizontal clipping. There are several cases: edge completely inside (add as-is), completely left (create vertical edge at x=0), completely right (create vertical edge at x=width), partially left, partially right, or spanning entirely across (create multiple edge segments).
   363→                    </p>
   364→
   365→                    <pre class="line-numbers" data-line="93-100,103-112,115-125"><code class="language-cpp">    int left = 0;
   366→    int right = fDevice.width();
   367→
   368→    if (p0.x &gt; p1.x) {
   369→        std::swap(p0, p1);
   370→    }
   371→
   372→    // IF edge is completely horizontally contained
   373→    if(p0.x &gt;= left &amp;&amp; p1.x &lt; right){
   374→        Edge inEdge = Edge(p0, p1, fDevice, dir);
   375→        if(inEdge.top &lt; inEdge.bottom){
   376→            edges.push_back(inEdge);
   377→        }
   378→        return;
   379→    }
   380→
   381→    // Line segment completely to the left
   382→    if (p1.x &lt; left) {
   383→        GPoint pp0 = {0, p0.y};
   384→        GPoint pp1 = {0, p1.y};
   385→        Edge leftEdge = Edge(pp0, pp1, fDevice, dir);
   386→        if(leftEdge.top &lt; leftEdge.bottom){
   387→            edges.push_back(leftEdge);
   388→        }
   389→        return;
   390→    }
   391→
   392→    // Line segment completely to the right
   393→    if (p0.x &gt;= right ) {
   394→        GPoint pp0 = {fDevice.width(), p0.y};
   395→        GPoint pp1 = {fDevice.width(), p1.y};
   396→
   397→        Edge rightEdge = Edge(pp0, pp1, fDevice, dir);
   398→        if(rightEdge.top &lt; rightEdge.bottom){
   399→            edges.push_back(rightEdge);
   400→        }
   401→        return;
   402→    }
   403→
   404→    // ... additional cases for partially clipped edges ...
   405→}</code></pre>
   406→
   407→                    <h4>Clipping Benefits</h4>
   408→                    <p>
   409→                        Clipping provides several benefits beyond correctness:
   410→                    </p>
   411→                    <ul>
   412→                        <li><strong>Memory safety</strong>: Prevents writing outside the bitmap buffer</li>
   413→                        <li><strong>Performance</strong>: Avoids rasterizing invisible pixels</li>
   414→                        <li><strong>Correctness</strong>: Handles edge cases at boundaries properly</li>
   415→                        <li><strong>Convexity preservation</strong>: Clipping a convex polygon against a convex viewport produces a convex result</li>
   416→                    </ul>
   417→                    <p>
   418→                        The convex polygon guarantee is crucial: because the input is convex and the clipping region is convex (a rectangle), the output is guaranteed to be convex. This means the simple scanline algorithm continues to work correctly on clipped geometry.
   419→                    </p>
