   230→        <h1>ALU Design</h1>
   231→        <p>Checkpoint 1 - Arithmetic Logic Unit</p>
   232→      </header>
   233→
   234→      <div className="container">
   235→        {/* Overview Section */}
   236→        <section className="section">
   237→          <h2>Overview</h2>
   238→          <p>
   239→            The <strong>Arithmetic Logic Unit (ALU)</strong> is the computational core of the processor. It performs arithmetic operations (addition, subtraction) and logical operations (AND, OR, shifts) on data. Every instruction that computes a result—whether it's adding two numbers, comparing values, or manipulating bits—relies on the ALU.
   240→          </p>
   241→          <p>
   242→            <strong>Input/Output Interface:</strong>
   243→          </p>
   244→          <ul>
   245→            <li><strong>Inputs:</strong> Two 32-bit data operands (<code>data_operandA</code>, <code>data_operandB</code>) and a 5-bit ALU opcode (<code>ctrl_ALUopcode</code>)</li>
   246→            <li><strong>Outputs:</strong> A 32-bit result (<code>data_result</code>) and three status flags: <code>isNotEqual</code>, <code>isLessThan</code>, and <code>overflow</code></li>
   247→          </ul>
   248→          <p>
   249→            <strong>Supported Operations:</strong> The ALU implements six fundamental operations: addition (ADD), subtraction (SUB), bitwise AND, bitwise OR, shift left logical (SLL), and shift right arithmetic (SRA). Each operation is selected by the 5-bit opcode provided by the control unit.
   250→          </p>
   251→        </section>
   252→
   253→        {/* Carry-Lookahead Adder Section */}
   254→        <section className="section">
   255→          <h2>Carry-Lookahead Adder</h2>
   256→          <p>
   257→            The heart of the ALU is the <strong>Carry-Lookahead Adder (CLA)</strong>, which performs fast 32-bit addition. Unlike a ripple-carry adder where the carry propagates sequentially through each bit position (resulting in O(n) delay), the CLA computes all carry bits in parallel using <strong>generate (G)</strong> and <strong>propagate (P)</strong> signals.
   258→          </p>
   259→
   260→          <h3>Why Carry-Lookahead?</h3>
   261→          <p>
   262→            In a ripple-carry adder, each bit position must wait for the carry from the previous position before computing its result. For a 32-bit adder, this creates a chain of 32 dependencies, making the critical path very long. The CLA breaks this chain by computing carries in logarithmic time: <strong>O(log n)</strong> instead of O(n).
   263→          </p>
   264→
   265→          <h3>Generate and Propagate Signals</h3>
   266→          <p>
   267→            The CLA uses two key signals for each bit position:
   268→          </p>
   269→          <ul>
   270→            <li><strong>Generate (G<sub>i</sub>):</strong> This bit position generates a carry regardless of the carry-in. This happens when both input bits are 1: <code>G<sub>i</sub> = A<sub>i</sub> AND B<sub>i</sub></code></li>
   271→            <li><strong>Propagate (P<sub>i</sub>):</strong> This bit position will propagate a carry-in to carry-out. This happens when exactly one input bit is 1: <code>P<sub>i</sub> = A<sub>i</sub> XOR B<sub>i</sub></code></li>
   272→          </ul>
   273→
   274→          <h3>Carry Equations</h3>
   275→          <p>
   276→            Using the generate and propagate signals, we can compute the carry-out for each bit position directly from the carry-in and the generate/propagate signals of previous positions. For a 4-bit block:
   277→          </p>
   278→          <ul>
   279→            <li><code>C<sub>1</sub> = G<sub>0</sub> + P<sub>0</sub>·C<sub>0</sub></code></li>
   280→            <li><code>C<sub>2</sub> = G<sub>1</sub> + P<sub>1</sub>·G<sub>0</sub> + P<sub>1</sub>·P<sub>0</sub>·C<sub>0</sub></code></li>
   281→            <li><code>C<sub>3</sub> = G<sub>2</sub> + P<sub>2</sub>·G<sub>1</sub> + P<sub>2</sub>·P<sub>1</sub>·G<sub>0</sub> + P<sub>2</sub>·P<sub>1</sub>·P<sub>0</sub>·C<sub>0</sub></code></li>
   282→            <li><code>C<sub>4</sub> = G<sub>3</sub> + P<sub>3</sub>·G<sub>2</sub> + P<sub>3</sub>·P<sub>2</sub>·G<sub>1</sub> + P<sub>3</sub>·P<sub>2</sub>·P<sub>1</sub>·G<sub>0</sub> + P<sub>3</sub>·P<sub>2</sub>·P<sub>1</sub>·P<sub>0</sub>·C<sub>0</sub></code></li>
   283→          </ul>
   284→
   285→          <h3>Two-Level CLA Structure</h3>
   286→          <p>
   287→            For a 32-bit adder, we use a <strong>two-level hierarchical design</strong>:
   288→          </p>
   289→          <ul>
   290→            <li><strong>Level 1:</strong> Eight 4-bit CLA blocks (bits 0-3, 4-7, 8-11, ..., 28-31)</li>
   291→            <li><strong>Level 2:</strong> A second CLA layer that computes the carry-in for each 4-bit block based on the group generate/propagate signals</li>
   292→          </ul>
   293→          <p>
   294→            This hierarchical approach keeps the logic depth manageable while achieving fast parallel carry computation.
   295→          </p>
   296→
   297→          <h3>Verilog Implementation - 4-bit CLA Block</h3>
   298→          <pre className="code-block">
   299→            <span className="comment">// 4-bit CLA block - Generate and Propagate</span>{'\n'}
   300→            <span className="keyword">wire</span> <span className="signal">[3:0] g</span>, <span className="signal">p</span>;<br/>
   301→            <span className="keyword">assign</span> <span className="signal">g</span> <span className="operator">=</span> <span className="signal">A</span> <span className="operator">&</span> <span className="signal">B</span>;           <span className="comment">// Generate: both bits are 1</span><br/>
   302→            <span className="keyword">assign</span> <span className="signal">p</span> <span className="operator">=</span> <span className="signal">A</span> <span className="operator">^</span> <span className="signal">B</span>;           <span className="comment">// Propagate: one bit is 1</span><br/>
   303→            <br/>
   304→            <span className="comment">// Carry lookahead equations</span><br/>
   305→            <span className="keyword">assign</span> <span className="signal">c</span>[<span className="number">1</span>] <span className="operator">=</span> <span className="signal">g</span>[<span className="number">0</span>] <span className="operator">|</span> (<span className="signal">p</span>[<span className="number">0</span>] <span className="operator">&</span> <span className="signal">c</span>[<span className="number">0</span>]);<br/>
   306→            <span className="keyword">assign</span> <span className="signal">c</span>[<span className="number">2</span>] <span className="operator">=</span> <span className="signal">g</span>[<span className="number">1</span>] <span className="operator">|</span> (<span className="signal">p</span>[<span className="number">1</span>] <span className="operator">&</span> <span className="signal">g</span>[<span className="number">0</span>]) <span className="operator">|</span> (<span className="signal">p</span>[<span className="number">1</span>] <span className="operator">&</span> <span className="signal">p</span>[<span className="number">0</span>] <span className="operator">&</span> <span className="signal">c</span>[<span className="number">0</span>]);<br/>
   307→            <span className="keyword">assign</span> <span className="signal">c</span>[<span className="number">3</span>] <span className="operator">=</span> <span className="signal">g</span>[<span className="number">2</span>] <span className="operator">|</span> (<span className="signal">p</span>[<span className="number">2</span>] <span className="operator">&</span> <span className="signal">g</span>[<span className="number">1</span>]) <span className="operator">|</span> (<span className="signal">p</span>[<span className="number">2</span>] <span className="operator">&</span> <span className="signal">p</span>[<span className="number">1</span>] <span className="operator">&</span> <span className="signal">g</span>[<span className="number">0</span>])<br/>
   308→            {'                      '}<span className="operator">|</span> (<span className="signal">p</span>[<span className="number">2</span>] <span className="operator">&</span> <span className="signal">p</span>[<span className="number">1</span>] <span className="operator">&</span> <span className="signal">p</span>[<span className="number">0</span>] <span className="operator">&</span> <span className="signal">c</span>[<span className="number">0</span>]);
   309→          </pre>
   310→        </section>
   311→
   312→        {/* ALU Operations Section */}
   313→        <section className="section">
   314→          <h2>ALU Operations</h2>
   315→          <p>
   316→            The ALU supports six operations, each selected by a 5-bit opcode. The operation results are computed in parallel, and a multiplexer selects the correct result based on the opcode.
   317→          </p>
   318→
   319→          <table className="ops-table">
   320→            <thead>
   321→              <tr>
   322→                <th>Opcode</th>
