   317→            {/* Tab 1: Edge Rasterization */}
   318→            <div className={`tab-panel ${activeTab === 'edge' ? 'active' : ''}`}>
   319→              <h3>Edge-Based Rendering</h3>
   320→              <p>
   321→                The graphics engine uses an edge-based scanline algorithm for all shape rendering. Every shape—rectangle, triangle, polygon, path—is first converted into a list of edges. Each edge represents one side of the shape, storing the top and bottom Y coordinates, the X position, and the slope (how X changes as Y increments).
   322→              </p>
   323→              <p>
   324→                The scanline algorithm then processes the frame buffer row by row. For each Y coordinate, it finds all active edges, calculates their X intersections, and fills between pairs of edges. This approach handles arbitrary shapes efficiently and naturally supports anti-aliasing in future extensions.
   325→              </p>
   326→
   327→              <h4>Edge Structure</h4>
   328→              <p>
   329→                Each edge stores its vertical span (top to bottom), its current X position, and its slope for efficient scanline traversal. The <code>dire</code> (direction) field tracks whether the edge runs left-to-right or right-to-left, which is crucial for the winding rule used in complex path rendering.
   330→              </p>
   331→
   332→              <pre className="code-block">{`class Edge {
   333→public:
   334→    int top, bottom;
   335→    float m, x;
   336→    int dire;
   337→    float left_x;
   338→
   339→    Edge(GPoint p0, GPoint p1, GBitmap fDevice, int dir) {
   340→       if (p0.y > p1.y) {
   341→            std::swap(p0, p1);
   342→        }
   343→        top = GRoundToInt(p0.y);
   344→        bottom = GRoundToInt(p1.y);
   345→        left_x = p0.x;
   346→        m = calculateSlope(p0, p1);
   347→
   348→        float b = calculateB(p0, p1);
   349→        x = b;
   350→
   351→        dire = dir;
   352→    }
   353→
   354→    float eval(int y){
   355→        return m * ((float)y + 0.5f) + x;
   356→    }
   357→
   358→private:
   359→    float calculateSlope(GPoint p0, GPoint p1) {
   360→        return (p1.x - p0.x) / (p1.y - p0.y);
   361→    }
   362→
   363→    float calculateB(GPoint p0, GPoint p1) {
   364→        return p0.x - (m * p0.y);
   365→    }
   366→};`}</pre>
   367→
   368→              <h4>Scanline Filling</h4>
   369→              <p>
   370→                The <code>eval()</code> method is the heart of edge rasterization. For any given Y coordinate, it calculates the corresponding X position by evaluating <code>m * (y + 0.5) + b</code>. The <code>+ 0.5</code> samples at the pixel center, which is essential for proper geometric accuracy.
   371→              </p>
   372→              <p>
   373→                During rendering, edges are sorted by Y position, then processed scanline by scanline. As each row is rendered, the algorithm finds pairs of edge intersections and fills the pixels between them. This approach works for any convex polygon and forms the foundation for more complex path rendering.
   374→              </p>
   375→            </div>
   376→
   377→            {/* Tab 2: Rectangle Drawing */}
   378→            <div className={`tab-panel ${activeTab === 'rect' ? 'active' : ''}`}>
   379→              <h3>Rectangle Rendering</h3>
   380→              <p>
   381→                Rectangles are the simplest renderable shape, but they demonstrate the core rendering pipeline. A rectangle is defined by four corner points, which are converted into four edges, then passed through the same scanline algorithm used for all shapes.
   382→              </p>
   383→              <p>
   384→                The <code>drawRect()</code> function converts a <code>GRect</code> into four <code>GPoint</code> vertices, then delegates to <code>drawConvexPolygon()</code>. This design means rectangles get all the benefits of the polygon renderer: clipping, transformation support, and complex blend modes.
   385→              </p>
   386→
   387→              <h4>Rectangle to Polygon Conversion</h4>
   388→              <pre className="code-block">{`void MyCanvas::drawRect(const GRect& rect, const GPaint& paint) {
   389→
   390→    GPoint p1 = {rect.left, rect.top};
   391→    GPoint p2 = {rect.right, rect.top};
   392→    GPoint p3 = {rect.right, rect.bottom};
   393→    GPoint p4 = {rect.left, rect.bottom};
   394→
   395→    GPoint src[4] = {p1, p2, p3, p4};
   396→    drawConvexPolygon(src, 4, paint);
   397→};`}</pre>
   398→
   399→              <h4>Convex Polygon Rendering</h4>
   400→              <p>
   401→                The <code>drawConvexPolygon()</code> function handles all convex shapes. It transforms points by the current transformation matrix, creates edges between consecutive vertices (wrapping from the last back to the first), clips those edges to the viewport, sorts them by Y coordinate, and finally fills the polygon using the scanline algorithm.
   402→              </p>
   403→
   404→              <pre className="code-block">{`void MyCanvas::drawConvexPolygon(const GPoint points[], int count, const GPaint& paint){
   405→
   406→    if(count < 3){
   407→        return;
   408→    }
   409→
   410→    GPoint dst[count];
   411→    GMatrix mat = ctm[ctm.size() - 1];
   412→
   413→    mat.mapPoints(dst, points, count);
   414→
   415→    std::vector<Edge> edges;
   416→
   417→    // Create array of edges
   418→    for(int i = 0; i < count; i++){
   419→        int nextIndex = (i + 1) % count;
   420→        Edge::clip(dst[i], dst[nextIndex], fDevice, edges);
   421→    }
   422→
   423→    if (edges.size() < 2){
   424→        return;
   425→    }
   426→
   427→    // Sort by top value
   428→    std::sort(edges.begin(), edges.end(), [](const Edge& a, const Edge& b) {
   429→        return a.top > b.top;
   430→    });
   431→
   432→    // ... blend mode selection and optimization ...
   433→
   434→    fillPolygon(edges, color, proc, fDevice);
   435→}`}</pre>
   436→
   437→              <p>
   438→                Note how the function creates edges by connecting each vertex to the next, with the last vertex wrapping back to the first via modulo arithmetic. This automatically closes the polygon. The edges are then clipped and sorted before being passed to the fill routine.
   439→              </p>
   440→            </div>
   441→
   442→            {/* Tab 3: Porter-Duff Blending */}
   443→            <div className={`tab-panel ${activeTab === 'blend' ? 'active' : ''}`}>
   444→              <h3>Alpha Compositing with Porter-Duff</h3>
   445→              <p>
   446→                The graphics engine implements the complete Porter-Duff compositing model with 12 blend modes. Porter-Duff blending defines how source pixels (S) combine with destination pixels (D) based on their alpha values, using the formula: <code>result = S_coef * S + D_coef * D</code>.
   447→              </p>
   448→              <p>
   449→                All blending operates on <strong>premultiplied alpha</strong>, where RGB channels are already multiplied by the alpha channel. This representation makes compositing math simpler and more efficient: instead of unpremultiply-blend-repremultiply on every pixel, we work directly with premultiplied values.
   450→              </p>
   451→
   452→              <h4>Src Over (Most Common Blend Mode)</h4>
   453→              <p>
   454→                The <code>src_over</code> blend mode is the default compositing operation used when drawing one image over another. It places the source pixel "over" the destination, with the destination showing through transparent areas. The formula is: <code>S + (1 - Sa) * D</code>, where Sa is the source alpha.
   455→              </p>
   456→
   457→              <pre className="code-block">{`// S + (1 - Sa)*D
   458→GPixel src_over_mode(GPixel src, GPixel dest){
   459→    int sa = GPixel_GetA(src);
   460→    int sr = GPixel_GetR(src);
   461→    int sg = GPixel_GetG(src);
   462→    int sb = GPixel_GetB(src);
   463→
   464→    int da = GPixel_GetA(dest);
   465→    int dr = GPixel_GetR(dest);
   466→    int dg = GPixel_GetG(dest);
   467→    int db = GPixel_GetB(dest);
   468→
   469→
   470→    int ba = sa + div255((255-sa)*da);
   471→    int br = sr + div255((255-sa)*dr);
   472→    int bg = sg + div255((255-sa)*dg);
   473→    int bb = sb + div255((255-sa)*db);
   474→
   475→    return GPixel_PackARGB(ba, br, bg, bb);
   476→}`}</pre>
   477→
   478→              <p>
   479→                The <code>div255()</code> helper function performs fast fixed-point division by 255, essential for performance since blending happens per-pixel. The alpha math <code>sa + div255((255-sa)*da)</code> produces the final alpha: fully opaque where source is opaque, blending source and destination alpha elsewhere.
   480→              </p>
   481→
   482→              <h4>Other Porter-Duff Modes</h4>
   483→              <p>
   484→                The engine supports all 12 standard Porter-Duff operators. Here are two more examples:
   485→              </p>
   486→
   487→              <pre className="code-block">{`// D + (1 - Da)*S
   488→GPixel dst_over_mode(GPixel src, GPixel dest){
   489→    int sa = GPixel_GetA(src);
   490→    int sr = GPixel_GetR(src);
   491→    int sg = GPixel_GetG(src);
   492→    int sb = GPixel_GetB(src);
   493→
   494→    int da = GPixel_GetA(dest);
   495→    int dr = GPixel_GetR(dest);
   496→    int dg = GPixel_GetG(dest);
   497→    int db = GPixel_GetB(dest);
   498→
   499→
   500→    int ba = da + div255((255-da)*sa);
   501→    int br = dr + div255((255-da)*sr);
   502→    int bg = dg + div255((255-da)*sg);
   503→    int bb = db + div255((255-da)*sb);
   504→
   505→    return GPixel_PackARGB(ba, br, bg, bb);
   506→}
   507→
   508→// (1 - Sa)*D + (1 - Da)*S
   509→GPixel xor_mode(GPixel src, GPixel dest){
   510→    int sa = GPixel_GetA(src);
   511→    int sr = GPixel_GetR(src);
   512→    int sg = GPixel_GetG(src);
   513→    int sb = GPixel_GetB(src);
   514→
   515→    int da = GPixel_GetA(dest);
   516→    int dr = GPixel_GetR(dest);
   517→    int dg = GPixel_GetG(dest);
   518→    int db = GPixel_GetB(dest);
   519→
   520→    int ba = div255((255-sa)*da) + div255((255-da)*sa);
   521→    int br = div255((255-sa)*dr) + div255((255-da)*sr);
   522→    int bg = div255((255-sa)*dg) + div255((255-da)*sg);
   523→    int bb = div255((255-sa)*db) + div255((255-da)*sb);
   524→
   525→    return GPixel_PackARGB(ba, br, bg, bb);
   526→}`}</pre>
   527→
   528→              <h4>Complete Blend Mode List</h4>
   529→              <p>The engine implements these 12 Porter-Duff operators:</p>
   530→              <ul>
   531→                <li><strong>clear</strong>: Result is transparent (0)</li>
   532→                <li><strong>src</strong>: Source replaces destination (S)</li>
   533→                <li><strong>dst</strong>: Destination unchanged (D)</li>
   534→                <li><strong>src_over</strong>: Source over destination (S + (1-Sa)*D)</li>
   535→                <li><strong>dst_over</strong>: Destination over source (D + (1-Da)*S)</li>
   536→                <li><strong>src_in</strong>: Source inside destination (Da*S)</li>
   537→                <li><strong>dst_in</strong>: Destination inside source (Sa*D)</li>
   538→                <li><strong>src_out</strong>: Source outside destination ((1-Da)*S)</li>
   539→                <li><strong>dst_out</strong>: Destination outside source ((1-Sa)*D)</li>
   540→                <li><strong>src_atop</strong>: Source atop destination (Da*S + (1-Sa)*D)</li>
   541→                <li><strong>dst_atop</strong>: Destination atop source (Sa*D + (1-Da)*S)</li>
   542→                <li><strong>xor</strong>: Exclusive or ((1-Sa)*D + (1-Da)*S)</li>
   543→              </ul>
   544→            </div>
